#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/694b10f1d1c18f25e079e9e7cc75e9cc457ef01d0c229f006308fd667108459b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/247c0a29294e61735b969d769d8ba3794c0b5e816c3968a8db087740eeb925d8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114997 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/e607bba88cc1f15abaa45fc3cf02110243433d60259c56805515ced373054ca0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822b1894-de17-5a39-b99c-38ebb8d1cf37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316047} {:answer/id #uuid "39875807-fe18-587b-8863-7346df9ccb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316048} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb8dbd8-ce6e-5f16-8b79-77be55ddeb4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316049} {:answer/id #uuid "610741b1-d059-5614-beb3-7a8eec8ec8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316050} {:answer/id #uuid "b78a6998-7559-5bed-9ec2-a1771dd67f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b78a6998-7559-5bed-9ec2-a1771dd67f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316051} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf90ac7-69d3-5fff-bf5a-ddb95afa0f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27d39cb2-4afd-51aa-ba9c-4ddc1b6ff266" :question/math-academy-id "q-130868" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12456346-b6a7-544d-ad76-1a777b9829e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ an $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316054} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e5bd9f-669b-5474-80ef-344d221820e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316055} {:answer/id #uuid "0f27e316-616c-5c96-aaea-3590c5a7f34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316056} {:answer/id #uuid "4afb4ada-2856-55cf-b68b-200a737f4bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316057} {:answer/id #uuid "2f438f20-12bf-5efc-9021-7b6f0bb59358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f438f20-12bf-5efc-9021-7b6f0bb59358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316058} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd8dd10c-0982-5496-9bfc-972d9275245b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d6c8932-9459-5228-9f76-3c00a801677b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131347" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1434626-7592-55e2-8c20-d8e0763453d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316061} {:answer/id #uuid "f220a910-1c8f-5ff5-9795-f881b26cc0fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316062} {:answer/id #uuid "2620eb0b-038f-5a5b-bc41-af20438232ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316063} {:answer/id #uuid "61a942d0-3f57-51be-934b-23a1d6f6f857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316064} {:answer/id #uuid "367cdc21-d31c-5c70-99dd-6a04769ac3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61a942d0-3f57-51be-934b-23a1d6f6f857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316064} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd019b54-0c3a-52c1-961b-b6d468db5ece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a618d522-1f89-5278-907e-8cb0fd49bf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-131381" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58574ec8-399a-5f6e-b6f2-d2f9038956ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316082} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5e7730-2ce7-5404-a715-71e79b625181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316083} {:answer/id #uuid "47ad9c3b-7721-57b0-9963-ebd1e32af9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316084} {:answer/id #uuid "da5b93c8-c6e6-57ab-8524-d917ba5b90fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316085} {:answer/id #uuid "ad716d96-451e-55ce-9a8c-2f9fafbe2b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47ad9c3b-7721-57b0-9963-ebd1e32af9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316084} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9db52fb-f11f-5324-a6c0-f787d3b28169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eacd4d73-2c00-54b5-9aa2-14a56b16b0f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-31871" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${B}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9285085c-ea68-5798-a388-d4d579f6640a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113950} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e6ccef-cb3a-59d2-968d-afa56f9e1b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113951} {:answer/id #uuid "d7eac3f1-c40c-5f81-885a-d59ee16580ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113952} {:answer/id #uuid "4c044149-6c33-5cb3-9c87-6a6f36e8846a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113953} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc0426c-9e05-5c6a-9ffb-1fac6dc087fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7eac3f1-c40c-5f81-885a-d59ee16580ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113952} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a82c2f-fee8-5718-a374-dde9723fde05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113949}] :question/id #uuid "90c835e0-83aa-519b-9995-715569dd5d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-43000" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2 \\cdot 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_{1}↔R_{2}$ and $R_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1}$?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5272bedb-f5cb-5248-a6e2-8c4780c44589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316116} {:answer/id #uuid "794a79e9-007b-53ea-b9e4-21cbacc01099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316117} {:answer/id #uuid "466f40a1-7d60-5832-81fb-9e44a5ac70a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316118} {:answer/id #uuid "789231b1-663a-52b6-8809-47c43cd39d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316119} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f7ca12-9fc7-5ffe-a773-210c53108e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3f7ca12-9fc7-5ffe-a773-210c53108e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316120} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27219ca-d5bc-5fb9-8469-6189d630c06d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5980ce24-2fb8-5a08-94d5-9900cc4fdcd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-43004" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $V$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $V$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${V}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3543849d-340b-5be2-b0c7-2c42ea36e3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1}, R_{1}:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113956} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb51950-3a14-5282-9aad-b325f327977e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{2}, R_{1}:=-2R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113957} {:answer/id #uuid "67149602-15e9-5144-9501-a4ef41b82042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{1}:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{2}, R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113958} {:answer/id #uuid "7d75c51c-8986-5d37-8f89-ee47ba2bb289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{2}, R_{1}:=R_{2} + R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113959} {:answer/id #uuid "7da931fa-98bd-5e8b-86ee-332fe29b5256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{1}:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{2}, R_{2}:=-R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3543849d-340b-5be2-b0c7-2c42ea36e3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1}, R_{1}:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113956} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7f6638d-d1d5-5a2a-a2a5-d0bd8079afa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113955}] :question/id #uuid "feb69023-a1fc-5fd4-a2b2-e338bb248f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-43006" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using the row operations as shown below. What are the corresponding row operations?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}___________ \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdd50191-b935-53c1-b729-05293a954016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316123} {:answer/id #uuid "ee539306-dc16-5c6a-ac64-c80f90300a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316124} {:answer/id #uuid "f36e00ee-0bd4-52b0-ac41-d5b0c68c8ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316125} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc52ad0-43a8-5d07-a2e3-026c0d7bb13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316126} {:answer/id #uuid "c11f12e8-e827-59dd-bc86-d2966f0728ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee539306-dc16-5c6a-ac64-c80f90300a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316124} :answer-field/id #uuid "feeb800c-51ce-52bf-9086-c68c25f4bdc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08503628-ebe1-533e-ab77-0800c6924f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-75448" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
